To add a link for every post, you'll need to modify the Nginx configuration to include a parameter in the HTML header or footer of your website. Here's an example of how you can do it:

**Method 1: Add a link in the HTML header**
```nginx
http {
    ...
    server {
        ...
        location / {
            root /usr/share/nginx/html;
            index index.html index.htm;
            try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;
            add_header x-fundu-link "<a href='/post/$uri'>Read More</a>";
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, we're adding an `add_header` directive to the `/` location block. This directive adds a custom header called `x-fundu-link` to the HTML page. The value of this header is an anchor tag with a link to the post page (e.g., `/post/$uri`). The `$uri` variable represents the URL path of the current page.

**Method 2: Add a link in the HTML footer**
```nginx
http {
    ...
    server {
        ...
        location / {
            root /usr/share/nginx/html;
            index index.html index.htm;
            try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;
            error_page 404 /404.html;
            error_page 500 502 503 504 /50x.html;
        }
        location = /404.html {
            root /usr/share/nginx/html;
            add_header x-fundu-link "<a href='/post/$uri'>Read More</a>";
        }
        location = /50x.html {
            root /usr/share/nginx/html;
            add_header x-fundu-link "<a href='/post/$uri'>Read More</a>";
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, we're adding the `add_header` directive to the `/404.html` and `/50x.html` error pages. This way, the link will be added to the footer of the error pages.

**Method 3: Use a templating engine**
If you're using a templating engine like Jinja2 or Handlebars, you can inject the link into the HTML template. For example, if you're using Jinja2, you can add a variable to the template like this:
```jinja
<link href="/post/{{ post_id }}">Read More</a>
```
Then, in your Nginx configuration, you can add a variable to pass the `post_id` to the template:
```nginx
http {
    ...
    server {
        ...
        location / {
            root /usr/share/nginx/html;
            index index.html index.htm;
            try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;
            set $post_id $uri;
            include 'template.jinja';
        }
    }
}
```
Make sure to adjust the variables and paths according to your specific use case.

Please note that these examples assume that you're using an HTML page as the default document. If you're using a different file format (e.g., Markdown), you'll need to adjust the configuration accordingly.<|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>

